#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68b74365-7af7-533c-a4b6-82b95ad8c598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295869} {:answer/id #uuid "4dac0845-ece5-5c93-ac78-e3107df5c303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295870} {:answer/id #uuid "978ca961-fc0f-5ab9-9bc4-26f6aa707375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295871} {:answer/id #uuid "b49dba43-1fc2-53b2-b7f9-3b4142fab53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295872} {:answer/id #uuid "3e965958-eaee-5339-9cf7-a5c18c0224a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "978ca961-fc0f-5ab9-9bc4-26f6aa707375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295871} :answer-field/id #uuid "b79b0e9e-ce07-5367-ae03-a5fb5855bf88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "116a80ae-5b7a-5a13-919a-314322a246f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143035" :question/problem "During a certain chemical reaction, the volume of gas $G$ increases with time. The rate of change of the volume of gas is proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds, since the reaction started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of gas at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a certain chemical reaction, the **volume** of gas $G$ **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the volume of gas is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of gas* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of gas *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8041938c-b394-5c87-9c43-eb0775333a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321141} {:answer/id #uuid "b2975091-274c-575a-9ca4-99f242ca3d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321142} {:answer/id #uuid "e23dcaae-343e-5c6f-ab58-e9e96baff3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2818cb-bca7-5dbf-ab48-fe00791e051c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321144} {:answer/id #uuid "28774533-52cb-5376-a3bd-b7e727f3538f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e23dcaae-343e-5c6f-ab58-e9e96baff3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca9088a-3574-5fb0-a2c2-bca04f47d657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4bd1c51-7cdb-5faf-a2e7-d35b9e8b6eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143077" :question/problem "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a food container decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the container $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a food container **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the temperature.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature $T$* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the temperature $T.$* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f3b892-f7f6-558e-81f6-37d50c51bdc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=-k{I}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281754} {:answer/id #uuid "22f3d1d6-5286-580f-908d-14fb13041de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=k{d}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281755} {:answer/id #uuid "479f7aaf-7152-5d55-a42e-ffcbc00b3aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{I}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281756} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a3e563-63ee-5583-a682-83a1701e6594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281757} {:answer/id #uuid "262ed2c0-4675-5f4b-819a-9fb07c163489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "262ed2c0-4675-5f4b-819a-9fb07c163489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281758} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ed3777e-048b-5ef6-b905-79fd03276bdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "503959ac-b932-5e4e-8061-f4b1c2072093" :question/math-academy-id "q-143078" :question/problem "The intensity $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the intensity of the light source $I?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **intensity** $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance** $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *intensity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the distance.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the intensity *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1409b4b7-07f9-5b67-83c9-b4ab0bdcbc7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366408} {:answer/id #uuid "8286a81c-ec59-52e1-9ed9-c8ce2c1953a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k{v}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366409} {:answer/id #uuid "00f48e47-279f-5def-940a-8d40692286ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=kv,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366410} {:answer/id #uuid "7da77f64-01ad-5ac3-a53f-bf4c1bb18337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-kv,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366411} {:answer/id #uuid "18dd537a-af28-5c07-bedd-7a4b36ed88c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da77f64-01ad-5ac3-a53f-bf4c1bb18337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-kv,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366411} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ece2767-21cf-5add-9ade-9d6a7a5599f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "307b1396-bf2f-5c6f-b4f9-47e505860659" :question/math-academy-id "q-38063" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a merry-go-round decreases at a rate that is proportional to the velocity at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the velocity at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **velocity** $v$ of a merry-go-round **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **velocity** at the moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *velocity* of a merry-go-round \nvaries. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *velocity.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}∝v.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the velocity *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-kv.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83e12d50-d127-5329-b350-e1b75dcce634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dH}{dt} = k\\sqrt{t}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127251} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f2144e-e83a-5ba8-a89a-b88f6dfc080d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dH}{dt} = kt, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127252} {:answer/id #uuid "18324c45-4e64-51ea-8ee8-5b717c7b749c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dH}{dt} = kH, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127253} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2acc1d-0a37-5489-8265-088e1fb88979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dH}{dt} = \\sqrt{kH}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127254} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8e5e57-fcef-50ba-a03c-98f686b90795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dH}{dt} =-kH, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f2144e-e83a-5ba8-a89a-b88f6dfc080d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dH}{dt} = kt, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127252} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbec041-1e7b-576d-a12e-34f2e18aa293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae5d1d81-2a58-5e33-81e9-cc4cd05cc40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38310" :question/problem "For the first year of a newborn's life, its height $H$ increases at a rate that is proportional to the time, in months, since birth.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height of the baby at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*For the first year of a newborn's life, its **height** $H$ **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time**, in months, since birth.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since birth. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$"}]}
